    "product_id": "b399f3a2-8090-4ead-b796-9e220827f076",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 200746\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1246 AM PDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dry and seasonable conditions will give way to increased \n  precipitation chances and a relative cool down on Monday and \n  Tuesday.\n\n* Temperatures will recover during the second half of the week with \n  low precipitation chances returning for our eastern zones as we \n  head into the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Saturday. \n\nDry, mild conditions and seasonable temperatures are on tap for \nsouthern Nevada, northwestern Arizona, and southeastern California \ntoday as we find ourselves under a transient ridge aloft. A low \npressure system will push inland and move through the area on Monday \nand Tuesday, bringing 30 to 60 percent chances of isolated-to-\nscattered showers and thunderstorms to Inyo County, the southern \nGreat Basin, and the Arizona Strip. There will be a slight uptick in \nprecipitable water (PW) with this system, which when combined with \nthe forcing associated with this low pressure system, will help to \nfuel the aforementioned isolated-to-scattered storms. The Mojave \nDesert will see PWs increase to around 0.8 to 1.0 inches, while PWs \nacross the southern Great Basin increase to around 0.4 to 0.7 \ninches. In addition to isolated showers and thunderstorms, this \nsystem will also bring slightly cooler temperatures to the area. \nHigh temperatures will cool around 3 to 6 degrees from today to \nTuesday, with overnight low temperatures dropping around 2 to 4 \ndegrees. \n\nAfter this low pressure system clears out on Wednesday we will see \n500 mb heights begin to rise as southwesterly flow sets up across \nthe area. These rising heights will allow for temperatures to \ngradually increase through the end of the week, while the \nsouthwesterly flow helps dry things back out. By Friday, \ntemperatures across the Mojave Desert will top out in the mid-90s to \nlower-100s, while temperatures across the southern Great Basin peak \nin the lower-80s to lower-90s. Ensemble guidance continues to hint \ntowards an uptick in moisture for the southern and eastern portions \nof our forecast area on Friday and Saturday. Currently, the best \nchances of precipitation associated with this moisture are around 10 \nto 20 percent in Mohave County on Saturday. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds \nfollow typical daily directional trends with speeds of less than 10 \nknots throughout the forecast period. An occasional wind gust to \naround 15 knots cannot be ruled out during the afternoon. VFR \nconditions will prevail with increasing mid and high cloud cover at \nor above 15kft. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds generally favor \ntypical daily directional trends for most locations over the next 24 \nhours. At KBIH, southerly winds with gusts between 15 and 20 knots \ndevelop in the afternoon, subsiding in the evening. VFR \nconditions will prevail with increasing mid and high level clouds \nfrom west to east tonight into Sunday. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Stessman\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
